    "chunk_id": "47660186-2c7b-4231-b4be-1f9ed79d3e03",
    "chunk_index": 11,
    "section_title": "CB8350EN/1/03.22 \u2013 Revised version",
    "text": "CB8350EN/1/03.22 \u2013 Revised version\n\nFor example, in Mexico, legumes in rotation with maize contribute organic matter and nitrogen that help boost maize yields by 25 percent. Zero tillage contributes to higher wheat yields, in the range of 6 to 10 percent, because it allows for timely sowing, leads to a better crop stand, and generates big savings on tractor operations, time and fuel. On the western, Indo-Gangetic plains, the adoption of zero- tillage in wheat production reduced farmer\u2019s costs per hectare by 20 percent and increased net income by 28 percent (FAO, 2016).",
    "paragraph_id": "6726d680-c56b-462e-8958-29f7a9d2e695",
    "paragraph_index": 0
  },
